  "body": "This has come up in a discussion about changing the markup of the [PHP ``highlight_string()`` and ``highlight_file()`` functions](https://github.com/php/php-src/pull/11913#issuecomment-1671532129).\r\n\r\nMy understanding is that the markup _should_ be as follows:\r\n```html\r\n<pre><code>\r\n<?php\r\nfunction foo() {\r\n    echo \"Hello world\";\r\n}\r\n?>\r\n</code></pre>\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd if one would to add syntactic colouring, one would go about by adding ``<span>`` tags as follows:\r\n```html\r\n<pre><code>\r\n<span class=\"tag\"><?php</span>\r\n<span class=\"keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"name\">foo</span>() {\r\n    <span class=\"keyword\">echo</span> <span class=\"string\">\"Hello world\"</span>;\r\n}\r\n<span class=\"tag\">?></span>\r\n</code></pre>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, the following [MDN note](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/code#notes):\r\n > The ``<code>`` element by itself only represents a single phrase of code or line of code.\r\n\r\nLeads to the interpretation that the following markup is acceptable:\r\n```html\r\n<pre>\r\n<code class=\"tag\"><?php</code>\r\n<code class=\"keyword\">function</code> <code class=\"name\">foo</code>() {\r\n    <code class=\"keyword\">echo</code> <code class=\"string\">\"Hello world\"</code>;\r\n}\r\n<code class=\"tag\">?></code>\r\n</pre>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, in the current living document there is no mention of doing the above and the examples provides for the [``<code>`` tag](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/text-level-semantics.html#the-code-element) and [``<pre>`` tag](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/grouping-content.html#the-pre-element) only demonstrate using a single ``<code>`` tag.\r\n\r\nMoreover, the markup used by the living document follows the markup of using only one ``<code>`` tag and using custom elements for individual pieces to syntactically highlight.\r\n\r\nI think it would be a good idea to clarify more how the ``<code>`` tag _should_ be used. As the current specification seems to be lacklustre on details.\r\n",
